WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.486 --> 00:00:17.230
Clarice Smith, and I sing and dance in a black vaudeville show.

2
00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:25.355
Vaudeville is the name performers have given to the many traveling shows that move from town to town

3
00:00:25.415 --> 00:00:26.656
throughout America.

4
00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:36.616
Vaudeville shows can have singers, dancers, musicians, jugglers, and magicians and they play in big cities and small

5
00:00:36.696 --> 00:00:40.919
towns. Some vaudeville people even perform in tents!

6
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:47.424
They rent out a farmer's field, put up a tent, and use oil lamps for stage lights.

7
00:00:48.124 --> 00:00:54.830
I've been in vaudeville since 1900, when I was 16, and I have performed in big shows and

8
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.772
small shows in a thousand different places.

9
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:05.182
Sometimes, when my husband Charlie and I are performing with a big show with lots of stars, we

10
00:01:05.222 --> 00:01:07.523
travel in our own railroad car.

11
00:01:08.843 --> 00:01:14.705
But we've been in small shows too, with just a piano player and a comedian, and then we

12
00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:17.186
drive from place to place in an old car.

13
00:01:18.686 --> 00:01:24.748
Growing up, I'd watch boys sing and dance on the street corner, and people would throw them pennies.

14
00:01:25.596 --> 00:01:29.759
It gave me the idea that I could make some money the same way.

15
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.645
We were poor and nobody took dance or music lessons, so I learned songs from the boys.

16
00:01:37.865 --> 00:01:41.768
I'd just sing and move my feet any way that I liked.

17
00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:48.914
People enjoyed my singing so much they threw me nickels, which I gave to my mama since she

18
00:01:48.954 --> 00:01:51.516
didn't make much money washing people's clothes.

19
00:01:52.988 --> 00:01:58.832
My lucky break came when one of the most popular shows in the South came to my little

20
00:01:58.872 --> 00:02:04.336
town. The show was Crackling Charlie and the Butterbeans, and they were wonderful.

21
00:02:05.337 --> 00:02:10.740
The Butterbeans were the best band I'd ever seen, and they could really get people dancing.

22
00:02:11.401 --> 00:02:13.903
I was 13 the first time I saw Charlie.

23
00:02:14.683 --> 00:02:18.786
He was the leader of the band and he stopped to see me dance and sing on the

24
00:02:18.846 --> 00:02:21.388
street. He gave me a dollar!

25
00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:27.821
The next time he came to town I was 14, and he gave me another dollar and a

26
00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:28.521
red ribbon.

27
00:02:29.722 --> 00:02:35.727
Two years later Charlie came to my house and asked Mama if I could join his show and

28
00:02:35.847 --> 00:02:36.828
marry him too.

29
00:02:37.589 --> 00:02:43.353
I knew that joining the show would be much better than doing laundry like Mama, so I told

30
00:02:43.394 --> 00:02:47.437
her that I was going with Charlie whether she agreed to let me go or not.

31
00:02:48.157 --> 00:02:51.060
Mama said yes, so Charlie and I got married.

32
00:02:52.412 --> 00:02:55.113
Suddenly I was in show business!

33
00:02:57.374 --> 00:03:02.656
Right away the piano player taught me some new songs and showed me how to sing them in

34
00:03:02.696 --> 00:03:05.477
a way that made people take notice.

35
00:03:05.497 --> 00:03:11.300
I soon realized that performing on stage was a lot different than singing on the street, so I

36
00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.741
paid close attention to my costumes.

37
00:03:14.541 --> 00:03:20.043
I sewed beads on a headband that I wore onstage, so that when the lights flashed on those

38
00:03:20.103 --> 00:03:22.064
beads, I sparkled!

39
00:03:23.452 --> 00:03:29.374
I sewed fringe on some of my dresses too, and I looked so wonderful, people couldn't stop clapping

40
00:03:29.414 --> 00:03:30.254
when I sang.

41
00:03:31.254 --> 00:03:37.776
But performing is hard work too, because in vaudeville you need to be a great entertainer and develop

42
00:03:37.856 --> 00:03:38.677
different talents.

43
00:03:39.217 --> 00:03:42.258
You have to learn new things and practice all the time.

44
00:03:42.798 --> 00:03:48.199
For example, it took Charlie and me a whole week to learn a new kind of tap dancing

45
00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:49.720
called the soft shoe.

46
00:03:51.784 --> 00:03:57.706
It was a popular dance that everybody wanted to see, so all the vaudeville performers started putting that

47
00:03:57.806 --> 00:03:59.066
dance in their shows.

48
00:04:01.167 --> 00:04:07.688
Taps are small pieces of metal on the bottoms of your shoes that make a nice click-click-click when

49
00:04:07.708 --> 00:04:08.229
you dance.

50
00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:14.270
For the soft shoe, we took the taps off and danced quietly on sand that we'd put on

51
00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.571
the floor ahead of time.

52
00:04:16.371 --> 00:04:20.632
Our feet made a nice swishing sound, and the audience loved it!

53
00:04:22.972 --> 00:04:28.935
Since it's against the law for whites and blacks to work or socialize together, there are separate theaters

54
00:04:29.076 --> 00:04:30.496
for white and black people.

55
00:04:31.737 --> 00:04:38.641
Charlie and I have performed at many famous black theaters in the country, such as the Douglass Theater

56
00:04:38.701 --> 00:04:45.525
in Macon, Georgia; the Liberty in Chattanooga, Tennessee; and even Gibson's Standard Theater in Philadelphia.

57
00:04:46.765 --> 00:04:51.408
Sometimes we're in town for a week and sometimes we stay only a day.

58
00:04:52.256 --> 00:04:57.459
But I always pack enough food to take with us when we're on the road, because black people

59
00:04:57.559 --> 00:04:59.660
aren't allowed to eat in most restaurants.

60
00:05:00.321 --> 00:05:06.504
The local hotels won't let us rent rooms either, so whenever we get to a town, we usually

61
00:05:06.585 --> 00:05:07.885
stay at someone's home.

62
00:05:08.746 --> 00:05:13.809
It costs thirty cents to see our show, which is a good price for such a lot of

63
00:05:13.909 --> 00:05:21.302
entertainment. Of course the performers only make about $30 a week each, because the theater owner usually takes

64
00:05:21.442 --> 00:05:22.803
most of admission price.

65
00:05:24.024 --> 00:05:27.867
Right now our show has a full band and a magician too.

66
00:05:28.668 --> 00:05:33.071
Most of the time, we do four performances a day, seven days a week.

67
00:05:33.992 --> 00:05:37.795
Recently Charlie and I have been doing a new comedy routine.

68
00:05:39.176 --> 00:05:42.479
We come out on stage dancing, and we look happy.

69
00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:48.384
Then I stop dancing and say, "Charlie, you talk too much.

70
00:05:49.076 --> 00:05:51.719
Sometimes I wish you were like a town clock."

71
00:05:52.619 --> 00:05:53.300
"Why, honey?"

72
00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:54.321
he asks.

73
00:05:54.341 --> 00:05:59.105
"Because then you'd only speak once an hour," I answer.

74
00:05:59.766 --> 00:06:01.888
The audience always laughs at that.

75
00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:04.871
Charlie and I do a few more dance steps.

76
00:06:05.671 --> 00:06:10.736
Then I stop and say, "Charlie, what's the difference between a teacher and an engineer?"

77
00:06:10.756 --> 00:06:12.598
"I don't know, honey.

78
00:06:12.898 --> 00:06:14.079
What is the difference?"

79
00:06:14.319 --> 00:06:14.960
he asks.

80
00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.842
"One trains the mind and the other minds the train," I answer.

81
00:06:20.522 --> 00:06:23.744
The audience loves to see a married couple tell jokes.

82
00:06:25.925 --> 00:06:31.687
Sometimes Charlie asks me if I want to settle down and have children, and he talks about getting

83
00:06:31.727 --> 00:06:33.948
a little place with enough land to farm.

84
00:06:34.748 --> 00:06:39.871
But times are hard and we don't have enough money saved right now, so I want to keep

85
00:06:40.011 --> 00:06:42.592
on singing and dancing as long as I can.

86
00:06:43.885 --> 00:06:48.891
If you ever want to have a good time, look for Crackling Charlie and his Clarice.

87
00:06:49.592 --> 00:06:54.839
We'll be looking for you, and the Butterbeans will be right behind us playing their hearts out.
